## triggers
21 19 2 1 message halm.well
# ONE EXAMINE PER CELL. Two `message` triggers on the same cell is a coin toss decided by file
# order — the engine's trig_at returns the first match — and the counter used to be three deep:
# `guild_hall_door` spanned x35-36 and sat on top of both clerk lines and the rival's door, so
# examining the counter gave you the doorway line and the guild book could never be signed. The
# chapter play-test found it on step 16. The board's "it is bare" state is NOT a second trigger any
# more either: it is the ALTERNATE on the one trigger (src/chapter01.h, CH_TRIG_COND), so the board
# is one place that says a different thing before the job exists.
35 20 1 1 message halm.guild_hall_door
34 20 2 1 zone guild_hall_door
33 20 1 1 message halm.job_sheet
32 20 1 1 message halm.job_sheet_2
# The counter is ONE trigger. Which of the clerk's two lines fires is the bell's business, not the
# map's: ch_clerk_line() swaps in `halm.clerk_signing_late` when the rings have run out, and both
# ids bind to the same flag, because the run is pressure and never a failure.
34 20 1 1 message halm.clerk_signing
36 20 1 1 message halm.rival_door
31 20 2 1 message halm.bell
20 26 3 1 message halm.lamp_charm
20 26 3 1 zone lamp_charm
20 27 3 1 message halm.lamp_charm_2
